-- title:
--   Lemma B.1 — repeated-squaring tail
-- statement:
--   Let $(\Omega,\mathcal F,\mu)$ be any probability space, let $k\ge0$ be an integer, and let $\alpha_i$ for $0\le i\le k$ be measurable real random variables. Assume that $\alpha_0$ is the constant $a$ with $0<a<1$. For each $0\le i<k$, assume $0\le\alpha_{i+1}\le\alpha_i$ almost surely and
--
--   $$\mathbb E[\alpha_{i+1}\mid\sigma(\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_i)]\le\alpha_i^2\quad\text{almost surely}.$$
--
--   The sigma algebra for $i=0$ is trivial. For every real $0<t<1$,
--
--   $$\Pr[\alpha_k\ge t]\le a^{\,k-\log_2\log_2(1/t)+\log_2\log_2(1/a)}.$$
--
--   The exponent is not clamped when it is negative. This repeated-squaring tail estimate supports the covering bound and, through it, Theorem B.3.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: random variables α_1 through α_k, deterministic α_0, and inequalities conditioned on their values → measurable real functions on an arbitrary probability space, with α 0 constant and step and conditional expectation inequalities almost everywhere; reason: the conditioning sigma algebra is the supremum of comaps for α_1 through α_i and is trivial at i = 0; boundedness follows from monotonicity, so no integrability premise is added
--   - encoding: the real power with exponent k − log_2 log_2(1/t) + log_2 log_2(1/α_0) → Real.rpow and Real.logb 2 with the exponent unchanged; reason: the bases and inner log arguments are positive under the stated hypotheses; k = 0 is the empty sequence case and also satisfies the inequality
-- source:
--   Vinayak M. Kumar, Geoffrey Mon, STOC 2027 submission #1578 (October 2026), Lemma B.1, printed p.34 = PDF p.34; https://github.com/sigact-stoc/stoc2027/releases/download/papers-2/1578.pdf; SHA-256 7cc3a39af88d6de0a57be33c5d8c2855020a7107b6d5e947fb47901273a80ed9

import Mathlib

open MeasureTheory

namespace ListHashFurstenberg.Main.Supplement

/-- Lemma B.1, p.34. The history at stage i is generated by α₁,…,αᵢ;
at i = 0 this is the trivial sigma algebra. Boundedness from the step hypotheses
makes the conditional expectations integrable; no extra integrability assumption is needed. -/
theorem lemma_B_1 {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω]
    (μ : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure μ]
    (k : ℕ) (α : ℕ → Ω → ℝ) (α₀ : ℝ)
    (hα₀ : 0 < α₀ ∧ α₀ < 1)
    (hzero : α 0 = fun _ => α₀)
    (hmeas : ∀ i ≤ k, Measurable (α i))
    (hstep : ∀ i < k, ∀ᵐ ω ∂μ, 0 ≤ α (i + 1) ω ∧ α (i + 1) ω ≤ α i ω)
    (hcond : ∀ i < k,
      (μ[α (i + 1) | ⨆ j : Fin i, MeasurableSpace.comap (α (j.val + 1)) (borel ℝ)])
        ≤ᵐ[μ] (fun ω => (α i ω) ^ 2))
    (t : ℝ) (ht : 0 < t ∧ t < 1) :
    μ.real {ω | t ≤ α k ω} ≤
      Real.rpow α₀ ((k : ℝ) - Real.logb 2 (Real.logb 2 (1 / t)) +
        Real.logb 2 (Real.logb 2 (1 / α₀))) := by sorry

end ListHashFurstenberg.Main.Supplement
